referenceSolutions.python:
def postageDrawerBossReceipt(readings, k):
    a=readings
    out=[]
    m=max(a,default=0)
    dp=[m+1]*(m+1)
    dp[0]=0
    for x in range(1,m+1):
        for coin in (1,4,9):
            if coin<=x: dp[x]=min(dp[x],dp[x-coin]+1)
    out=[dp[x] for x in a]
    return sum((i+1)*x for i,x in enumerate(out))

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int postageDrawerBossReceipt(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int m=0;for(int x:a)m=Math.max(m,x);int[]dp=new int[m+1];Arrays.fill(dp,m+1);dp[0]=0;for(int x=1;x<=m;x++)for(int coin:new int[]{1,4,9})if(coin<=x)dp[x]=Math.min(dp[x],dp[x-coin]+1);for(int x:a)out.add(dp[x]);}
    int answer=0;for(int i=0;i<out.size();i++)answer+=(i+1)*out.get(i);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

For each nonnegative postage amount, produce the smallest number of stamps needed using unlimited denominations 1, 4 and 9. Zero postage uses zero stamps.

Return the weighted sum of the derived sequence: multiply its first value by 1, its second by 2, and so on, then add the products. Return 0 for an empty sequence.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n + M); space O(n).

